QB.push(
{id:1601,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An agent loop decides a task is finished like this:\n```python\nif any(b.type == 'text' for b in response.content):\n    return final_text(response)\n```\nIt passes every test. In production about a third of runs return a chatty half-answer such as \"I'll look that up for you now\" and the requested work is never done. What is the defect?",
o:["A. `max_tokens` is too low, so the tool call is being truncated away before the loop sees it","B. The model is ignoring the tools because their descriptions are too short","C. An assistant turn may contain both `text` and `tool_use` blocks, so the presence of text does not mean the turn is final","D. `text` blocks are only emitted once the model has finished, so the check is correct and the tools must be misconfigured"],a:2,
e:"Claude routinely narrates before acting, so a single assistant turn can hold a `text` block *and* one or more `tool_use` blocks. Inferring completion from content shape therefore discards real tool calls. The authoritative signal is **`stop_reason`**: `tool_use` means run the tools and continue, `end_turn` means the turn is genuinely final. The transferable principle is to branch an agent loop on the response's control metadata, never on which block types happen to be present.",
w:"A — truncation surfaces as `stop_reason` of `max_tokens`, and the text preamble would still be accompanied by a `tool_use` block. B — the model is calling the tool; the loop is throwing the call away. D — the documented example response shows a `text` block followed by a `tool_use` block in the same turn, which is precisely this case."},

{id:1602,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Claude has emitted a `tool_use` block for one of your own client tools and is waiting for you to run it. What is that response's `stop_reason`?",
o:["A. `tool_use`","B. `pause_turn`","C. `end_turn`, with the pending call described in `stop_details`","D. `stop_sequence`"],a:0,
e:"A response that leaves a client tool call waiting on you always carries **`stop_reason` of `tool_use`**. `pause_turn` is a different situation: a server-side tool loop that hit its own iteration limit, which you continue by sending the assistant content back. The docs are explicit that a response leaving a client `tool_use` block outstanding never has `pause_turn`. Knowing which value means \"your turn to execute\" is what lets the loop branch correctly.",
w:"B — `pause_turn` signals that a server-tool turn paused mid-flight, not that your client tool is pending. C — `end_turn` means Claude finished, and `stop_details` is populated only when `stop_reason` is `refusal`. D — `stop_sequence` means one of your configured `stop_sequences` fired."},

{id:1603,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A research agent mixes its own client tools with the server-side web search tool. Its loop is `while stop_reason == 'tool_use'`, and anything else returns the text. On broad queries users get answers that stop mid-thought. Logs show those runs ended with `stop_reason` of `pause_turn`. What is the correct handling?",
o:["A. Retry the identical request; `pause_turn` is a transient server condition","B. Replace web search with a client tool, since server tools cannot be used inside an agent loop","C. Treat `pause_turn` as terminal but raise `max_tokens` so the turn completes in a single pass","D. Append the assistant response to `messages` and issue another request so Claude can continue the paused turn"],a:3,
e:"**`pause_turn`** means a long-running turn that the API was driving itself, such as a multi-step web search, reached its internal iteration limit. The documented handling is to add the assistant's response to your `messages` array and make another request with the same `tools`, letting Claude resume. Any loop that uses server tools must branch on it explicitly, because the run is neither finished nor waiting on you. Treating it as terminal is exactly how you get confidently truncated answers.",
w:"A — retrying from scratch discards the work already done inside the paused turn and can loop indefinitely. B — server tools are fully usable inside agent loops; they simply add `pause_turn` to the set of stop reasons you must handle. C — `max_tokens` bounds output length, not the server-side iteration limit that caused the pause."},

{id:1604,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An agent detects refusals by substring-matching phrases such as \"I can't help with that\". It misfires in both directions: a tool result quoting that sentence trips the detector, and a genuine refusal phrased differently slips through and is treated as a final answer. What is the correct mechanism?",
o:["A. Inspect the first `text` block for a leading apology and treat that as a refusal","B. Branch on `stop_reason` of `refusal` and read the `category` carried in `stop_details`","C. Set `output_config.effort` to `max` so that refusals are returned as a 400 error instead","D. Check for an empty `content` array, which is how refusals are delivered"],a:1,
e:"Refusals are reported structurally: **`stop_reason` of `refusal`**, with `stop_details` carrying an open-set `category` naming the policy that triggered it. `stop_details` is `null` for every other stop reason, so its presence is itself diagnostic. Detect on the field, not the prose, and record the category in your termination reason. A refused request can often be served by retrying on a different Claude model, which is a decision only a reliable detector can drive.",
w:"A — phrasing is not a contract; it varies by model, context and language. C — `output_config.effort` steers reasoning depth, and refusals are never delivered as HTTP errors. D — a refusal response still carries content; emptiness is not the signal."},

{id:1605,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"At iteration 19 of a long agent run the response returns `stop_reason` of `model_context_window_exceeded`. The loop's generic error handler retries the identical request three times with backoff, then fails the task. What should it do instead?",
o:["A. Lower `max_tokens` and retry, since the output cap is what was exceeded","B. Treat the response as truncated and shrink the history before continuing","C. Retry with longer exponential backoff; the condition clears when load drops","D. Split the request into two calls that each carry the same history"],a:1,
e:"The context window bounds the request **plus** everything generated, including thinking tokens, so a long accumulated history can fill it during generation. `model_context_window_exceeded` says the response was cut off for that reason: treat it as **truncated**, then reduce the prompt before continuing by clearing or compacting old tool results, tightening tool projections, or delegating bulk exploration. Because the outcome is a function of the input, a retry of the same request reproduces it exactly.",
w:"A — lowering `max_tokens` does not shrink the prompt; the window was filled by accumulated history plus generation. C — this is not a load condition; 429 `rate_limit_error` and 529 `overloaded_error` are. D — two calls each carrying the same oversized history both exceed the same window."},

{id:1606,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"Why should an agent loop include a default branch over `stop_reason` rather than an exhaustive set of equality checks?",
o:["A. Because `stop_reason` is null while streaming and only resolves at `message_stop`","B. Because a response may carry several `stop_reason` values at once","C. Because the documented versioning policy permits the `stop_reason` enum to grow, so a client can meet a value it was not written for","D. Because SDKs translate `stop_reason` into locale-specific strings"],a:2,
e:"The currently documented values are `end_turn`, `max_tokens`, `stop_sequence`, `tool_use`, `pause_turn`, `refusal` and `model_context_window_exceeded` — and the **versioning policy explicitly allows the set to be extended**, so new values are not a breaking change. A loop with no default branch silently falls through when one appears. The right default logs the unrecognised value and terminates with an explicit, diagnosable reason rather than returning nothing.",
w:"A — while streaming, the final `stop_reason` arrives in `message_delta`; a completed message always carries one. B — exactly one value is returned per response. D — these are stable API identifiers, not localised display text."},

{id:1607,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An agent's dispatcher is written as:\n```python\nif r.stop_reason == 'tool_use':\n    ...\nelif r.stop_reason == 'max_tokens':\n    ...\nelif r.stop_reason == 'end_turn':\n    return text_of(r)\n```\nA small fraction of runs return `None`, and the caller raises a `NoneType` error with no useful context. What is the most robust fix?",
o:["A. Re-raise inside the `end_turn` branch so the caller sees a real exception","B. Convert the chain to a match statement over the same three values","C. Wrap the call site in a retry, so a `None` result is simply retried","D. Add a final `else` that logs the unhandled `stop_reason` and terminates with an explicit reason"],a:3,
e:"`refusal`, `pause_turn` and `model_context_window_exceeded` all fall straight through this chain, and future values will too. A **default branch** converts an unknown-unknown into a diagnosable termination: capture the value, the iteration index and the accumulated results, then return a typed reason. The general rule for agent loops is that every path must end in a reason you can act on, because an unexplained `None` surfaces as a crash somewhere far from its cause.",
w:"A — the `end_turn` branch is the one that already works; the fall-through happens for values it never tests. B — a match over the same three values leaves the identical hole. C — retrying reproduces a deterministic refusal or context overflow and buries the cause even deeper."},

{id:1608,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"In a hand-written loop, once you have run the tools Claude requested, what must be appended to `messages` before the next request?",
o:["A. The response's full `content` array as an `assistant` message, then a `user` message whose content holds the `tool_result` blocks","B. A `user` message with the `tool_result` blocks only; the assistant turn is retained server-side","C. A `tool`-role message containing the results, followed by the assistant turn","D. A `user` message containing the results as plain text summarising each tool's output"],a:0,
e:"Each iteration appends **two** messages: the assistant turn exactly as returned, then a `user` message carrying the `tool_result` blocks. Replaying the assistant `content` array verbatim preserves the `tool_use` blocks whose `id` values the results must reference, plus any `thinking` blocks the model expects back unchanged. There is no `tool` role in this API, and the API is stateless, so whatever you replay *is* the conversation.",
w:"B — nothing is retained server-side; omitting the assistant turn leaves the results with no `tool_use` block to pair against. C — the Claude API has no `tool` role; results ride inside `user` messages as `tool_result` blocks. D — plain text destroys the `tool_use_id` pairing and the structural signal that this content is tool output."},

{id:1609,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A refactor moves the line that appends the assistant turn so it now runs *after* the append of the tool-result message. The next request fails with an error stating that `tool_use` ids were found without `tool_result` blocks immediately after them. Why?",
o:["A. The assistant turn must be the last message in the array on every request","B. `tool_result` blocks may only appear in the first `user` message of a conversation","C. Tool result blocks must immediately follow the assistant turn that requested them, with no message in between","D. `tool_use_id` values expire as soon as a second message is appended"],a:2,
e:"Pairing is **positional as well as referential**: a `tool_result` must sit in the message immediately following the assistant turn containing its `tool_use` block. Reversing the appends puts the results before the call, so the ids in the assistant turn are left unanswered and the request is rejected. The practical defence is to build the two appends as one atomic step in the loop, so ordering cannot drift during refactors.",
w:"A — the last message on a tool-continuation request is the `user` message carrying the results. B — tool results appear wherever a tool call preceded them, at any depth of the conversation. D — ids do not expire; the failure here is purely positional."},

{id:1610,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"To make transcripts readable, a developer builds the results message as a text block reading \"Results for iteration 4:\" followed by the `tool_result` blocks. Every such request now returns 400. What is the rule being broken?",
o:["A. Text blocks are not permitted in a message that also carries tool results","B. `tool_result` blocks must come first in the content array; any text may only follow them","C. The text block must carry the same `tool_use_id` as the results it introduces","D. Only one content block is allowed per user message when tools are enabled"],a:1,
e:"In the user message that answers tool calls, **every `tool_result` block must precede any text**. Text before the results is a documented 400. A trailing text block after all results is legal and is the supported way to add a follow-up instruction, with one caveat: if the assistant turn also called a server tool whose result has not arrived, the message must contain nothing but `tool_result` blocks, because trailing text ends the turn early.",
w:"A — text is allowed, but only after every `tool_result` block in the array. C — `tool_use_id` is a field of `tool_result` blocks and is meaningless on a text block. D — a user message may carry many blocks; the constraint is ordering, not count."},

{id:1611,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A loop injects a bookkeeping `user` message containing the word \"continue\" between the assistant's tool-calling turn and the message carrying the results, so its transcript viewer renders each iteration as a discrete exchange. Every iteration now 400s. What is the cause?",
o:["A. Nothing may sit between the assistant turn that requested tools and the user message carrying the results","B. The word `continue` collides with a reserved control token","C. Two consecutive `user` messages are rejected by the API","D. The bookkeeping message must use the `assistant` role instead"],a:0,
e:"The formatting rule is absolute: **you cannot include any message between the assistant's tool-use message and the user's tool-result message**. The interposed turn strands the `tool_use` ids, which is the 400. Presentation concerns belong in your own telemetry layer, keyed on the iteration index and `tool_use_id`, never in the wire history — the message array is a protocol artefact, not a display model.",
w:"B — message text contains no reserved words; the request is rejected on structure. C — consecutive same-role turns are combined into a single turn rather than rejected, which is a common misconception worth unlearning. D — an interposed assistant message breaks the pairing just as badly, and would also misrepresent the model's own output."},

{id:1612,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An agent's tool definitions total roughly 4,000 tokens and are billed on every iteration. To cut cost, a team stops sending `tools` on all requests after the first. Multi-step tasks now stop after one tool call. What is the correct optimisation?",
o:["A. Send `tools` only on requests where the previous `stop_reason` was `end_turn`","B. Move the tool schemas into the `system` parameter, where they are cheaper","C. Set `tool_choice` type `none` on follow-up requests, keeping the definitions but skipping their cost","D. Keep `tools` on every request and place a `cache_control` breakpoint on the last tool definition so the prefix is read from cache"],a:3,
e:"Statelessness means **tool definitions must be resent on every request**, and they are billed as input tokens each time plus a fixed tool-use system prompt overhead. Removing them removes the capability, not the cost driver. Caching the tools prefix is the real lever: one write at 1.25x base input, then reads at 0.1x. Tools sit first in the cacheable prefix (tools then system then messages), which makes that breakpoint the highest-value one in an agent request.",
w:"A — that is backwards; `end_turn` is where the task has already finished. B — schemas placed in `system` are still billed and are no longer callable tools. C — `tool_choice` type `none` still transmits and bills every definition while forbidding any call."},

{id:1613,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A response arrives with `stop_reason` of `max_tokens`, and its final block is a `tool_use` whose `input` JSON is incomplete. The executor attempts to run it, throws a JSON parse error, and returns that as `is_error`. The agent then retries and truncates again. What is the correct handling?",
o:["A. Return the parse error with `is_error` true and let the model shorten its arguments","B. Discard the partial block and re-request with `tool_choice` type `any`","C. Concatenate the next response's first block onto the partial JSON before parsing","D. Do not execute a truncated call; re-issue the request with a higher `max_tokens`"],a:3,
e:"An incomplete `tool_use` block is not a model mistake, it is your output cap cutting the response mid-generation. The documented handling is to **retry the request with a higher `max_tokens`** to obtain the full tool call. A truncated `input` object must never be executed or repaired by guesswork, because a half-written JSON value can invert meaning. The loop should therefore treat `max_tokens` alongside a trailing `tool_use` block as its own branch.",
w:"A — feeding back a parse error blames the model for your cap and invites the same truncation next time. B — forcing tool use does not raise the output ceiling. C — `partial_json` fragments are concatenated across `input_json_delta` events within a single streamed block, never across separate responses."},

{id:1614,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"E",
q:"An assistant turn contains three `tool_use` blocks. One of the three raises an exception, so the executor returns results for the two that succeeded and writes the failure to its log. The next request returns 400. What should it have sent?",
o:["A. A `tool_result` for all three ids, with `is_error` true on the one that failed","B. Nothing until the failed tool has been retried to success, then all three results","C. A fresh conversation without the assistant turn, re-asking the original question","D. The two successes now and the third result in the following user message"],a:0,
e:"**Every `tool_use` id must be answered in the next user message.** An unanswered id is a 400, so silence about a failure is not an option. Returning it as a `tool_result` with `is_error` true and an informative message keeps the history valid and hands the model something it can act on: retry differently, choose another tool, or report the task as blocked. Failure is data in an agent loop, not an exception to be swallowed.",
w:"B — unbounded retries stall the loop and are simply wrong for non-retryable failures such as permission denied. C — discarding the turn throws away the model's plan and the two calls you already paid for. D — splitting results across messages leaves ids unanswered on the first request and teaches the model to stop making parallel calls."},

{id:1615,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A turn contains four `tool_use` blocks. Because two of them write to the same record, the executor runs the batch sequentially. Call 2 fails, so calls 3 and 4 are never attempted. What belongs in the results message?",
o:["A. Results for calls 1 and 2 only; calls that were never attempted are omitted","B. Four `tool_result` blocks: call 1's output, call 2's error, and errors for 3 and 4 stating they were not executed","C. One `tool_result` for call 1 plus a text block explaining what happened to the rest","D. Four blocks, with 3 and 4 carrying empty `content` and `is_error` false"],a:1,
e:"The documented pattern is explicit: if you choose not to run a call, **still return a `tool_result` for it with `is_error` true and a brief explanation** such as \"Not executed: the preceding write_file call failed.\" That satisfies the pairing requirement and, more importantly, tells the model the true state of the world so its next plan is grounded. Sequential execution of interdependent writes is a legitimate strategy; abandoning ids is not.",
w:"A — unanswered ids are a validation failure, so this request never reaches the model. C — a text block cannot answer a `tool_use` id, and must in any case follow all results. D — an empty success is a fabricated result: the model will proceed believing those writes happened."},

{id:1616,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A loop appends one `user` message per tool result. Nothing errors, but over several weeks the average number of tool calls per assistant turn drifts from 2.4 down to 1.0 and end-to-end latency doubles. What is happening?",
o:["A. The API rejects extra user messages after the first, so later results are ignored","B. Consecutive same-role messages are merged, so results after the first are dropped","C. The history now demonstrates one result per message, which teaches the model to stop making parallel calls","D. Parallel tool use must be re-enabled on each turn through `tool_choice`"],a:2,
e:"The docs name this directly: a separate `user` message for each tool result is the **wrong** formatting, and it teaches Claude to avoid parallel calls. Your own conversation history is the strongest few-shot example the model has, so a transcript full of single-result turns becomes a demonstration to imitate. The correct shape is one `user` message containing all `tool_result` blocks together, matched by `tool_use_id`, results before any text.",
w:"A — nothing is rejected; every request succeeds, which is why the regression is silent. B — same-role turns are combined rather than dropped, so no content is lost. D — parallel tool use is on by default; you switch it off with `disable_parallel_tool_use` inside `tool_choice`, and it needs no per-turn re-enabling."},

{id:1617,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"When a single assistant turn contains several `tool_use` blocks, what does the API require about how you execute them?",
o:["A. They must be executed concurrently, in the order in which they appear","B. Nothing: the execution strategy is yours, and only the result formatting is prescribed","C. They must be executed in reverse order, so the last call's result appears first","D. They must be executed one at a time, awaiting each result before starting the next"],a:1,
e:"The docs state that the API **does not prescribe an execution order**: run the calls concurrently, sequentially, or in any mix that suits your tools. The recommendation is judgement-based — independent read-only operations are usually safe in parallel for lower latency, while tools with side effects, shared state or ordering requirements are better run sequentially. What *is* prescribed is the reply: one `tool_result` per `tool_use`, all in the next `user` message, results before text, matched by `tool_use_id`.",
w:"A — concurrency is a latency choice rather than a requirement, and it is unsafe for tools with side effects. C — there is no reverse-order rule; results are matched by `tool_use_id`, not by position. D — serialising every batch is permitted but discards the latency benefit of independent reads."},

{id:1618,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An agent's turn contains `create_invoice` and `apply_credit_note`, both mutating the same ledger row. The executor runs every batch with `asyncio.gather`. Occasionally the credit note is applied against a stale balance. What is the appropriate fix?",
o:["A. Add `disable_parallel_tool_use` to the request permanently","B. Return the two results in separate user messages so the model serialises future calls","C. Execute calls that share mutable state sequentially, keeping concurrency for independent read-only calls","D. Instruct the model in the system prompt never to request these two tools in the same turn"],a:2,
e:"Because the API leaves execution strategy to you, **dispatch policy is your executor's responsibility** and should be keyed on tool identity: parallel for independent reads, sequential where tools share state, have side effects, or imply an order. Group the turn's calls into a safe schedule, then return all results together in one user message. This is the model-proposes, code-authorises pattern applied to concurrency rather than to permissions.",
w:"A — that removes parallelism for every tool including safe reads, paying latency everywhere to fix a two-tool problem. B — separate result messages degrade parallel calling generally and do nothing to make this turn's writes safe. D — a prompt instruction is advisory; the model will occasionally batch them anyway and the corruption returns."},

{id:1619,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"What does setting `is_error` true on a `tool_result` block do?",
o:["A. Aborts the turn and returns a 400 to the caller","B. Causes the API to retry the tool call automatically","C. Marks the conversation as failed, so the next response's `stop_reason` is `refusal`","D. Tells Claude that the tool execution failed, so it can adapt, retry differently, or explain the failure"],a:3,
e:"`is_error` is **information for the model, not control flow**. Claude incorporates the failure into its next step, which is why the message content matters: write instructive errors such as \"Rate limit exceeded. Retry after 60 seconds.\" rather than \"failed\". For invalid tool inputs, Claude typically retries two or three times with corrections before apologising. Server tools are different — the API handles their errors itself, so you never set `is_error` for them.",
w:"A — the request is valid; an error-flagged result is ordinary content. B — there is no server-side retry of client tools; retries belong to your executor or to the model. C — `refusal` is a policy decline and is unrelated to tool failure."},

{id:1620,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"E",
q:"A browser-automation tool returns a PNG screenshot. The team currently base64-encodes the bytes into a sentence of text inside the tool result, and the agent's visual reasoning is poor. What is the correct shape for that result?",
o:["A. Set the `tool_result` block's `content` to an array holding a short `text` block and an `image` block carrying the screenshot source","B. Send the image as a separate `user` message immediately after the results message","C. Attach the image to the assistant turn alongside the `tool_use` block","D. Return a URL in the text and let the model fetch the image itself"],a:0,
e:"`tool_result.content` may be a string **or an array of content blocks, including images**, which is what makes screenshot-driven agents possible. Base64 pasted into prose is just thousands of tokens of noise: the bytes must sit in an `image` block so they are processed as visual tokens. One related caching note for loops: toggling images present and absent between requests invalidates the messages cache, so intermittent screenshots cost a prefix re-write.",
w:"B — a separate message breaks the rule that results immediately follow their calls, and the image would not be attached to the `tool_use_id`. C — the assistant turn is the model's own output; you never author blocks into it. D — the model does not browse on its own; a URL only helps if it is an image source or a tool retrieves it."},

{id:1621,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"Which content-block types may appear inside a `tool_result` block's `content` array?",
o:["A. `text` only","B. `text` and `tool_use`","C. `text`, `image`, `document` and `search_result`","D. Any block type, including `thinking` and `compaction`"],a:2,
e:"A tool result may carry a **string or an array of content blocks**, and the permitted block types are `text`, `image`, `document` and `search_result`. That is what lets a tool hand back a screenshot, a PDF, or citation-bearing search hits directly rather than flattening everything into prose. The design consequence is that a tool's return type is a real interface decision in an agent loop: pick the block type that preserves the structure the model needs, instead of stringifying it and hoping the model reconstructs it.",
w:"A — the array form exists precisely so results can carry non-text content. B — `tool_use` is the model's own request block and never appears inside a result. D — `thinking` and `compaction` blocks belong to assistant turns, not to tool output."},

{id:1622,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"What happens if a `tool_result` block omits `content` altogether?",
o:["A. The request fails with `400 invalid_request_error`, because `content` is required","B. The API substitutes placeholder text describing the missing result","C. The block is discarded, leaving the `tool_use` id unanswered","D. The request is valid, since `content` is optional, and Claude simply receives no information from that call"],a:3,
e:"`content` is **optional** on a `tool_result`; only `tool_use_id` must be present, so an empty result is documented as legal. The damage is therefore behavioural rather than a validation error: pairing succeeds, nothing errors, and the model is left with no basis for its next step, which is exactly what produces near-identical repeat calls. Always return something decision-bearing, even if that is only \"0 rows matched\" plus the filter used.",
w:"A — `content` is documented as optional; `tool_use_id` is the required field. B — no placeholder is inserted for a result you left empty; placeholders appear only when server-side context editing clears an older result. C — the block is not discarded; it satisfies the pairing requirement for that id."},

{id:1623,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An executor gathers parallel calls with `asyncio.as_completed` and then pairs results to calls with `zip(tool_uses, results)`. No request ever 400s, but the agent occasionally reports the weather of one city under the name of another. What is the defect?",
o:["A. `as_completed` swallows exceptions, so some results are silently lost","B. Results must be matched to calls by `tool_use_id`; completion order is not request order","C. The `tool_use` blocks arrive in a non-deterministic order within the response","D. Parallel calls require `disable_parallel_tool_use` in order to keep positions stable"],a:1,
e:"**`tool_use_id` is the pairing key.** Because a positional zip still emits the right number of results with valid, unique ids, the request passes validation and the mismatch is completely silent — the worst failure class, since the agent answers confidently with swapped data. The transferable rule: never let ordering carry identity when the protocol already supplies an explicit identifier. Build a dict from id to result and look each one up.",
w:"A — exceptions are surfaced by `as_completed`; the defect here is misattribution, not loss. C — the response's `content` array order is fixed; it is your completion order that varies. D — disabling parallelism would mask the bug at a latency cost, and the positional assumption would still be wrong the moment two calls returned together."},

{id:1624,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"After a socket timeout, an agent's retry wrapper rebuilds the request by appending the previous iteration's user message again alongside the current one. The retried request returns 400, reporting a `tool_result` that matches no `tool_use`. What is the explanation?",
o:["A. A `tool_result` must pair with a `tool_use` id in the immediately preceding assistant turn, so a replayed id from an earlier turn has nothing to match","B. `tool_use` ids are single-use tokens that the server invalidates after first use","C. Duplicate ids are permitted, but the second silently overwrites the first","D. The 400 is caused by the timeout itself rather than by the message shape"],a:0,
e:"Unmatched `tool_use_id` values are a **400**, and the match is evaluated entirely within the single request you send, because the server holds no conversation state. Transport retries must therefore resend a byte-identical request rather than accumulate messages. The robust construction is to make the request a pure function of your checkpointed history, so a retry cannot mutate it: build the payload once, hand that same object to the retry, and never append inside the retry path. This also keeps the cached prefix byte-identical, so the retry still hits cache instead of paying for a fresh write.",
w:"B — the server keeps no per-conversation state, so there is nothing to invalidate; matching happens inside each request. C — a duplicated or unmatched id is a validation error, not a silent overwrite. D — a timeout yields no such error; the shape of the rebuilt request does."},

{id:1625,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"R",f:"H",
q:"An assistant turn contains a client `tool_use` block and a `server_tool_use` block that has no result block yet. What may the following `user` message contain?",
o:["A. Only the `tool_result` blocks for the client tools; trailing text would end the turn early and fail the request","B. The client results plus a text block instructing Claude to resume the server tool","C. A `tool_result` for the client call and a synthesised result for the server call","D. Nothing: you must wait and re-poll before replying"],a:0,
e:"When a server tool call is still outstanding, the reply must contain **only `tool_result` blocks**, and you keep the same `tools` array. Text after the results ends the turn early, and for a server tool Claude invoked directly the request then fails with a 400 naming the unresolved tool. The API runs the pending server tool on your next request, and the following response begins with its result block. Trailing text is legal only when the turn called client tools alone.",
w:"B — that trailing text is exactly what triggers the failure in this situation. C — you never author a server tool's result; the API produces it. D — there is nothing to poll; the continuation happens on your next request."},

{id:1626,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An agent service returns `{ok: false}` to its callers. Operations cannot decide whether to re-queue a failed job: some failures are transient, some are policy refusals, and some are the iteration cap firing on genuinely hard work. What change most directly solves the caller's problem?",
o:["A. Raise the iteration cap so fewer runs fail","B. Log the full message history for every failed run so engineers can read it","C. Return the last `stop_reason` verbatim as the failure code","D. Return a typed termination reason — completed, cap reached, budget exhausted, refusal, tool failure — alongside any partial results"],a:3,
e:"A boolean collapses outcomes whose remedies are opposites. A cap-reached run is worth resuming from its checkpoint; a refusal should not be retried on the same model; a permission failure needs a human. Making the **termination reason part of the return contract**, paired with partial results and a checkpoint reference, is what lets the calling system decide without reading transcripts. Undiagnosable success/fail flags are the single most common reason agent operations cannot be automated.",
w:"A — a higher cap changes the failure rate, not the caller's inability to classify what happened. B — transcripts help a human debug one run; they give the calling system no machine-readable decision. C — `stop_reason` describes only the final model response and cannot express your own guards, such as a wall-clock deadline or a spend cut-off."},

{id:1627,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An agent capped at 25 iterations calls tools that each take 20 to 40 seconds. Behind a gateway with a 60-second response timeout, callers see 504s at around iteration 2, while the loop keeps running server-side and spending money on a response nobody will receive. What is the right guard?",
o:["A. Reduce the iteration cap to 2 so the loop cannot outlive the gateway timeout","B. Move the loop behind a retry queue that re-runs it from the start whenever a 504 occurs","C. Track a wall-clock deadline, check it before each iteration, and return partial results with a timeout reason before the gateway gives up","D. Stream the response so the gateway keeps seeing bytes and does not time out"],a:2,
e:"An iteration cap bounds **steps**, not time, and tool latency is the variable that actually breaches upstream timeouts. A production loop needs a budget on every exhaustible dimension — iterations, tokens or cost, and wall-clock — each evaluated *before* dispatching the next step and each producing its own distinguishable termination reason plus whatever partial results exist. Work that legitimately needs longer belongs on an asynchronous job model rather than a synchronous request.",
w:"A — a cap of 2 cripples every task to accommodate the slowest tool, and one 50-second tool still breaches the deadline. B — restarting from zero repeats the spend, hits the same timeout, and re-runs any side effects. D — keeping the socket warm hides the symptom while leaving the run with no bound on how long it may take."},

{id:1628,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A task completes in 8 iterations. The initial prompt plus tool definitions is about 9,000 tokens, and each tool result adds roughly 1,500. Finance asks why the run billed close to 100,000 input tokens rather than about 21,000. What is the explanation?",
o:["A. Tool definitions are billed once per conversation, so the excess must be output tokens","B. The API is stateless, so each iteration re-sends the whole accumulated history; the total is the sum of those growing prefixes","C. Input is double-counted when tools are present: once for the schema and once for the call","D. `max_tokens` is reserved and billed as input on every request"],a:1,
e:"Cost in an agent loop is an **arithmetic series, not a single prompt**: iteration k pays for everything produced before it, so tokens grow with the square of the iteration count. That reframes the optimisation targets — cache the stable prefix (write 1.25x, read 0.1x), keep tool results narrow, clear or compact stale results, and delegate bulky exploration so its bytes never enter this history. Cutting the number of requests alone barely helps if each one carries more.",
w:"A — definitions are billed as input on every request, which is part of the growth, and output here is small. C — there is no double count; the growth comes from re-sending history. D — `max_tokens` is an output ceiling, billed only on tokens actually generated, and it does not even factor into output-token rate limits."},

{id:1629,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A budget guard sums `input_tokens` plus `output_tokens` from each response and multiplies by the list input price. With prompt caching enabled it overstates spend severalfold, so long tasks are killed while still well inside budget. How should the guard compute cost?",
o:["A. Sum only `output_tokens`, since cached input is free","B. Call `/v1/messages/count_tokens` before each request and budget from that","C. Divide the input total by the iteration count to remove the double counting","D. Weight `cache_read_input_tokens` at about 0.1x and `cache_creation_input_tokens` at about 1.25x of base input, adding uncached `input_tokens` at 1x"],a:3,
e:"`usage` reports the cache components separately for exactly this reason. In a long loop, cached reads dominate the input volume, so treating every input token as full price mis-triggers the guard on the runs you most want to finish. Track the four fields and apply the documented multipliers — write 1.25x (2x for the 1-hour TTL), read 0.1x. Avoid hard-coding currency amounts; the **multipliers** are the stable part of the model.",
w:"A — cached reads are discounted rather than free, and uncached input still dominates a cold request. B — `count_tokens` returns one estimated `input_tokens` figure, does not exercise caching, and says nothing about output. C — the repeated input is genuine billing, not an artefact; each iteration really does re-process the prefix, discounted only where it hits cache."},

{id:1630,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A loop guard aborts a run when the same tool name appears three times consecutively. It now kills legitimate paginated reads at page 3, while an agent alternating two useless queries still runs to the cap. How should repeat detection be keyed?",
o:["A. On the total number of calls per tool per task","B. On elapsed time rather than on repetition","C. On the tool name plus canonicalised arguments, treating a repeat as no-progress only when the arguments are unchanged","D. On a system-prompt instruction asking the model not to repeat calls"],a:2,
e:"The identity of a tool call is **name plus arguments**, canonicalised — keys sorted, whitespace normalised, values coerced consistently. Advancing a cursor changes the arguments and is progress; re-issuing a byte-identical query is not. Prefer escalation to a hard abort: on the first repeat, return an `is_error` result naming the repetition and suggesting a different approach, and only then terminate with a distinguishable no-progress reason.",
w:"A — a per-tool ceiling still punishes legitimate deep pagination and still permits unproductive alternation below the ceiling. B — a clock bound is a valuable separate guard but cannot distinguish productive from unproductive calls. D — a prompt request is advisory; loop safety must be enforced by the executor."},

{id:1631,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"An agent loop reaches its iteration cap before the task is finished. What should it return?",
o:["A. The work completed so far, an explicit cap-reached reason, and what remains outstanding","B. An exception, so the caller retries the whole task from the beginning","C. The model's most recent `text` block, presented as the final answer","D. Nothing, so that no partially-correct output can mislead a consumer"],a:0,
e:"Cap exhaustion is a **planned outcome, not a crash**. Graceful degradation returns the findings gathered, a typed reason, the outstanding steps, and ideally the checkpoint reference so a follow-up resumes rather than restarts. The reason belongs in the return value and not only in the logs, because the caller is the component that must decide whether to resume, escalate or abandon. The dangerous option is presenting an interrupted run's last utterance as an answer: mid-plan narration is indistinguishable from a conclusion once it leaves the loop.",
w:"B — an exception discards work you have already paid for and invites a full re-run into the same cap. C — the last `text` block is mid-plan narration and will read to the consumer as a finished answer. D — silence is equally undiagnosable and throws away useful output; the fix is labelling results as partial, not hiding them."},

{id:1632,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"Which response data lets an agent loop accumulate a per-task token budget?",
o:["A. `max_tokens` and `stop_reason` on each response","B. `input_tokens`, `output_tokens`, `cache_creation_input_tokens` and `cache_read_input_tokens` in `usage`","C. The `anthropic-ratelimit-*` response headers","D. The `iterations` field returned by `count_tokens`"],a:1,
e:"Each response's **`usage`** object reports the four token components, and the loop accumulates them across iterations to enforce a budget and to attribute cost per step. Recording them per iteration rather than only as a total is what lets you see which step's tool result caused a jump, and the cache fields are what let you price the run correctly, since reads and writes are not billed at base input. One trap: when server-side compaction runs, the top-level `input_tokens` and `output_tokens` exclude the compaction pass, so a complete figure requires summing every entry in `usage.iterations`.",
w:"A — `max_tokens` is a cap you set rather than consumption, and `stop_reason` carries no counts. C — those headers report remaining rate-limit quota, which is neither per-task nor a cost. D — `count_tokens` returns a single estimated `input_tokens` value and is a pre-flight estimate, not accounting."},

{id:1633,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"Where must an agent loop's iteration cap be enforced?",
o:["A. By instructing the model in the system prompt to take no more than N steps","B. By setting `max_tokens` low enough that the model runs out of room","C. In the loop code, as a counter checked before each request","D. By switching `tool_choice` to type `none` after N tool calls"],a:2,
e:"The model is **not a control plane**: it cannot reliably count its own steps and has no visibility of your budget, so the cap belongs in deterministic code that runs before each request. Explaining the budget in the prompt is still useful, because it helps the model plan within the limit, but it cannot be the enforcement point. Same principle that governs permissions: the model proposes, your code authorises.",
w:"A — a step instruction is advisory and its influence decays as the history grows. B — a low `max_tokens` truncates responses mid-call, producing unparseable tool inputs rather than a clean stop. D — that forbids further calls but is itself something you must implement in code, and it leaves the loop free to keep issuing requests."},

{id:1634,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"The SDK Tool Runner helper drives the tool-use loop for you. Which parameter bounds how many iterations it will run?",
o:["A. `max_tokens`","B. `stop_sequences`","C. `disable_parallel_tool_use`","D. `max_iterations`"],a:3,
e:"Tool Runner handles multi-call responses, result formatting and the loop itself, and **`max_iterations`** is its loop guard; you drive it to completion by awaiting the runner or calling its run-until-done method. Because it builds the `tool_result` blocks and pairs the ids for you, the whole family of pairing 400s — unanswered ids, text before results, interposed messages — simply stops occurring. Adopting it removes the boilerplate, not the engineering: token and wall-clock budgets, repeat-call detection, checkpointing and termination reasons remain yours to add around it.",
w:"A — an output-length ceiling for one response, unrelated to loop length. B — stop sequences truncate a single response's text. C — that limits a response to one tool call; it does not bound how many responses the runner requests."},

{id:1635,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An agent's guard runs the tools first, then adds up the accumulated token spend, and aborts if the budget is exceeded. A run whose budget is blown on the iteration that requested `issue_refund` has already issued the refund by the time the guard fires. What is the correct structure?",
o:["A. Evaluate every guard — budget, deadline, iteration count — before dispatching any tool call, and abort with a reason instead of executing","B. Wrap the refund tool in a try/finally that rolls the refund back when the guard fires","C. Reduce the budget so that the guard fires one iteration earlier","D. Move the guard check inside each tool's own implementation"],a:0,
e:"Guards must gate the **irreversible** step, so the per-iteration order is: check guards, dispatch tools, append results, checkpoint, request again. A refund executed after the budget was exhausted cannot be undone by accounting. Where a side effect cannot be perfectly gated — the process can always die mid-call — the complementary controls are recording intent before acting and making the call idempotent so a replay is harmless.",
w:"B — a rollback assumes the payment system supports compensating a completed refund, which is usually untrue. C — a tighter budget changes when the ordering bug bites, not whether it does. D — every tool would then need its own copy of the loop's accounting, and a tool executing inside a parallel batch cannot see the batch total."},

{id:1636,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"E",
q:"Every tool in an agent returns the string `failed` with `is_error` true whenever anything goes wrong. The agent responds by re-calling the same tool with the same arguments two or three times, then apologising to the user. What is the fix?",
o:["A. Return an empty result instead, so the model does not see a failure at all","B. Return what went wrong and what to try next, for example that the rate limit was exceeded and the call may be retried after 60 seconds","C. Set `is_error` false so the model treats the message as ordinary data","D. Strip the error and let the executor's own retry logic handle everything silently"],a:1,
e:"The docs put this plainly: write **instructive** error messages instead of generic ones like \"failed\", including what happened and what Claude should try next. The model's next action is a function of what the result says, so an opaque failure leaves repetition as the only available move. A good failure payload names the category, states whether a retry could help, and offers an alternative path.",
w:"A — an empty result is even less informative and produces the same blind repetition. C — mislabelling a failure as data invites the model to reason from `failed` as though it were a value. D — silent transport retries are correct for a flaky socket, but a genuine failure hidden from the model leaves it planning against a world state that never happened."},

{id:1637,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An agent's tools return free prose on failure, such as \"Something went wrong talking to the CRM, sorry!\". The agent retries permission failures indefinitely and gives up instantly on transient ones. What is the structural fix?",
o:["A. Return a structured envelope on every failure: a category, whether the call is retryable, and any alternative available","B. Return the HTTP status code as the entire result and let the model infer the rest","C. Map every failure to a single `unavailable` category so the model's behaviour is consistent","D. Move retry decisions into the system prompt as a per-tool list of rules"],a:0,
e:"A model's recovery strategy can only be as good as the classification it receives. The documented shape, the same discipline MCP prescribes for tool errors, is an envelope carrying **category, retryability and alternatives**: authentication, validation, rate limit, not found, unavailable; retryable true or false with a hint such as retry after 60 seconds; and a suggested different path. Prose forces the model to infer retryability from tone, which is why the two failure classes are being handled backwards.",
w:"B — a bare status is opaque to a planner and ambiguous across tools; a 403 from a proxy and a 403 from the CRM demand different responses. C — flattening every failure into one category destroys precisely the distinction that decides whether a retry can ever succeed. D — per-tool rules in the prompt duplicate what the failure itself should carry and drift as tools change."},

{id:1638,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A tool wrapper catches all exceptions and returns `{status: 'ok'}` so the agent never gets stuck. Runs now finish cleanly and users are told their tickets were filed. Support finds no tickets. Why is this the worst of the available failure modes?",
o:["A. It wastes tokens, because the agent continues past the point where it should have stopped","B. It makes the model's belief diverge from reality and removes every signal that would trigger retry, escalation or alerting","C. It violates the requirement that every `tool_use` id be answered","D. It causes the loop to terminate on `refusal` rather than `end_turn`"],a:1,
e:"A loud failure is recoverable: the model can retry, switch tools, or report the task as blocked, and your monitoring sees an error rate. A **fabricated success is unrecoverable by construction** — the agent reports completion, the caller trusts it, nothing errors, and the divergence is discovered only by an external party. The rule is that a wrapper may translate a failure but must never invent a payload it did not receive.",
w:"A — the token cost is trivial beside reporting work that never happened. C — the id is answered; the answer is simply false, which is why nothing errors. D — the run ends normally on `end_turn`, and that is exactly the problem."},

{id:1639,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An agent's HTTP tool wrapper retries every non-2xx response five times with backoff. Tasks that hit a `403` now take 40 seconds longer before failing, and a malformed-argument `400` is retried identically five times. How should the retry policy be set?",
o:["A. Retry nothing in the executor; return every failure to the model, which is better placed to decide","B. Retry everything, but cap the total at two attempts","C. Retry on the basis of the HTTP method rather than the status","D. Retry only failures that could plausibly succeed unchanged, such as 429 with `retry-after`, 5xx and socket timeouts, and return 400 and 403 immediately labelled non-retryable"],a:3,
e:"Retryability is a property of the failure, not a global setting. A 429 or 529 is transient and self-clearing; a 403 authorisation failure and a 400 schema failure are deterministic and will fail identically forever, so retrying them only consumes the run's wall-clock budget. Surface those to the model at once, labelled **non-retryable**, so it can change an argument, pick another tool, or declare the task blocked rather than waiting on a hopeless backoff.",
w:"A — absorbing transport flakiness in the executor is far cheaper than a model round trip and keeps noise out of the context. B — a lower cap still spends attempts on failures that cannot succeed and still hides the distinction from the model. C — the method tells you whether a retry is *safe*, which matters for idempotency, but not whether it could succeed."},

{id:1640,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A payments agent calls `charge_card`. The HTTP call times out after the charge was in fact captured; the executor retries and the customer is billed twice. The team proposes never retrying on timeout. What is the sound design?",
o:["A. Return the timeout to the model and let it decide whether to charge again","B. Retry only on connection-refused, never on timeout","C. Give the side-effecting call an idempotency key derived from the task step, so a repeat with the same key is recognised and returns the original result","D. Wrap the charge in a database transaction on the agent's side"],a:2,
e:"A timeout is genuinely ambiguous: success and failure look identical to the caller. Retries in agent systems are **inevitable and arrive from three directions** — the executor retrying an ambiguous failure, the model re-issuing a call it believes failed, and a resumed checkpoint replaying an in-flight iteration. So deduplication must live at the boundary that owns the effect, keyed deterministically from the `tool_use` id or task step so every replay produces the same key.",
w:"A — the model has no more information than your executor and cannot distinguish a captured charge from a lost request; asking it converts a systems problem into a guess. B — declining to retry turns a double charge into an unresolved one, and does nothing about the model or a resume replaying the call. D — a local transaction cannot roll back a capture that already completed inside the payment processor."},

{id:1641,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Why do side-effecting agent tools need to be idempotent?",
o:["A. Because the Messages API resends the whole history each iteration, which re-executes prior calls","B. Because parallel `tool_use` blocks are always executed twice for redundancy","C. Because retries are unavoidable: executors retry ambiguous failures, models re-issue calls they believe failed, and resumed checkpoints replay an in-flight iteration","D. Because results flagged with `is_error` are retried automatically by the API"],a:2,
e:"Three independent mechanisms replay a call, and none of them is optional in a real agent: transport-level retry of an ambiguous failure, the model deciding to try again after an unclear result, and crash recovery replaying the iteration that was in flight. **Idempotency keys, conditional writes or a dedupe window** are what make those replays safe. Worth unlearning the adjacent misconception: resending history re-bills tokens but never re-runs a tool.",
w:"A — resending history costs tokens; execution happens only in your code, once per dispatch. B — parallel calls run once each; concurrency is not redundancy. D — the API does not retry client tools at all."},

{id:1642,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An agent calls `create_ticket` without the required `priority` field. The executor raises a `KeyError`, which the loop returns as a full stack trace with `is_error` true. The agent tries twice more and gives up. What is the right response, immediately and structurally?",
o:["A. Substitute a default priority so the call succeeds","B. Return a short error naming the missing parameter and its allowed values, and remove the class of failure with a clearer parameter description or `strict: true` on the tool","C. Force the call with `tool_choice` type `tool` so the model must retry the same tool","D. Paste the tool's full JSON schema into the system prompt"],a:1,
e:"Two horizons. Now: `Error: Missing required 'priority' parameter (one of low, normal, high)` is documented as enough for Claude to retry with the field filled in, typically within two or three attempts. Permanently: invalid inputs signal a description gap, and **`strict: true`** applies constrained decoding to the tool's `input_schema` so inputs cannot violate it. A stack trace buries the single fact the model needed under hundreds of tokens.",
w:"A — inventing a priority makes a business decision nobody authorised and conceals the schema gap. C — forcing the tool says nothing about what was wrong with the arguments. D — schemas already reach the model through `tools`; duplicating them in `system` pays for them twice and drifts out of sync."},

{id:1643,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A tool retries an unreliable dependency internally: six attempts with exponential backoff, up to 90 seconds, before returning. The agent's 120-second wall-clock deadline now expires inside a single tool call, so runs end with no partial results and no indication of which step was slow. What is the fix?",
o:["A. Bound executor-side retries so one call cannot consume the run's remaining time, propagate the deadline into the call, and surface the failure to the loop once that step's share is spent","B. Remove the deadline, since agents are long-running by nature","C. Return the first failure immediately with no executor retry at all, leaving retries entirely to the model","D. Run every tool call on a background thread so the deadline is never blocked"],a:0,
e:"Retries here are a good idea with a bad default. Every layer that can wait must receive a **share of the deadline**, so the loop retains enough time to record partial results and terminate diagnosably. A workable shape is a per-call timeout of the smaller of the tool's default and the remaining budget minus a reserve, retrying only while that holds, then returning a retryable-labelled error that states attempts and elapsed time.",
w:"B — an unbounded run breaches upstream timeouts and has no cost ceiling; the deadline is what makes partial results possible at all. C — pushing every transient fault to the model spends a full round trip of tokens and latency on something the executor absorbs in milliseconds. D — detaching the call leaves the loop unable to answer while the work carries on consuming the dependency and the budget."},

{id:1644,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A database tool returns the driver's full 500-line stack trace on failure, roughly 4,000 tokens, and that result is then resent on every subsequent iteration of the run. What should the tool return instead?",
o:["A. Truncate every tool result to 200 characters","B. Set `is_error` false so the trace is treated as ordinary data","C. Remove the failing tool from `tools` for the remainder of the run","D. A one-line category plus the actionable detail, keeping the full trace in your own logs behind a reference id"],a:3,
e:"Two costs compound: the model cannot act on a trace, and those tokens are re-billed on every later iteration. A tool result is a **model-facing interface**, not a debugging channel, so return the decision-relevant projection — `Error: query timed out after 30s; narrow the date range. ref=err_8123` — and keep the trace in telemetry keyed by that reference. The reference id preserves debuggability without paying for it in context.",
w:"A — a blanket character cap mangles legitimate results and can cut a trace mid-token while preserving none of the useful part. B — mislabelling a failure as data encourages the model to reason from it as though it were a value. C — mutating `tools` mid-run invalidates the entire cached prefix and withdraws a capability the task may still need."},

{id:1645,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"Which statement about `is_error` on a `tool_result` block is correct?",
o:["A. The API validates that the content genuinely describes an error and 400s otherwise","B. It must also be set for server tool failures, such as a failed web search","C. It suppresses the result from the model's context while keeping the pairing valid","D. It is an unvalidated hint to the model; the API does not inspect the content"],a:3,
e:"`is_error` is **advisory metadata for the model**, with no server-side validation of the content — which is precisely why a careless wrapper can lie in either direction, flagging a valid empty result as an error or returning a fabricated success. Server tools differ: Claude handles their errors internally and surfaces them in its own result blocks, so you never set `is_error` for them. Removing content from context is a separate mechanism entirely.",
w:"A — no such validation exists; the content is free text or content blocks. B — server tool errors are handled by the API rather than by your executor. C — an error-flagged result is fully visible to the model, and that visibility is the entire point."},

{id:1646,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A search tool runs successfully but matches no rows. How should that call be answered?",
o:["A. `is_error` false, with content stating that zero rows matched and echoing the filter that was applied","B. `is_error` true, with content reading `no results`","C. Omit `content` entirely, so the model understands nothing was found","D. `is_error` true, with content repeating the query so the model retries it"],a:0,
e:"A successful query with an empty result set is **not a failure**, and mislabelling it teaches the model to treat a valid answer as something to route around or retry. What actually determines behaviour is informativeness: `0 rows matched status='open' AND owner='ak' within the last 30 days` tells the model which constraint to relax. A bare \"no results\" or an omitted `content` field supports no decision, which is what produces near-identical repeat calls.",
w:"B — flagging a valid empty answer as an error misclassifies it, and `no results` still leaves the model with nothing to change. C — an omitted `content` field is legal but conveys less than nothing: the call is answered and the model learns only silence. D — echoing the query while calling it an error is an explicit invitation to repeat the identical call."},

{id:1647,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An agent repeatedly passes `region: \"EMEA-North\"` to a tool whose schema permits only `emea`, `apac` and `amer`. The team's current fix is a paragraph in the system prompt listing the valid values. What is the stronger fix?",
o:["A. Reject the call in the executor and return the valid values, relying on the model's two or three correction attempts","B. Put the allowed values in the parameter's schema as an `enum` and set `strict: true` on the tool so inputs are constrained at decode time","C. Normalise the value in the executor with a fuzzy match","D. Set `tool_choice` type `any` so the model commits to a tool and gets its arguments right"],a:1,
e:"**Strict tool use** applies constrained decoding to the tool's `input_schema`, so a value outside the enum cannot be generated at all. That removes the failure class rather than correcting it after the fact, and it costs no round trip. A prompt paragraph is advisory and competes for attention with a history that grows every iteration, whereas the schema is structural and travels with the tool definition on every request.",
w:"A — returning the valid values is a sound fallback, but it spends an extra round trip per occurrence on something the schema can prevent outright. C — fuzzy normalisation silently guesses intent and will eventually map a value to the wrong region. D — forcing tool use dictates that a tool is called, not that its arguments are valid."},

{id:1648,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"R",f:"H",
q:"A long-running agent enables server-side clearing of old tool results through `context_management`. What must the client do with its own copy of the conversation?",
o:["A. Mirror each clear locally by deleting the same blocks, or the next request is rejected","B. Call `count_tokens` after every response to discover which blocks were removed","C. Nothing: edits are applied server-side before the prompt reaches the model, and the client keeps the full unmodified history","D. Replace each cleared result with the placeholder text the API used, byte for byte"],a:2,
e:"Context editing is applied **server-side, before the prompt reaches Claude**, and the documentation is explicit that your client maintains the full, unmodified conversation history with no need to sync. The API substitutes placeholder text for each cleared result so the model knows something was removed, and reports what it did under `applied_edits` with fields such as `cleared_tool_uses` and `cleared_input_tokens`. Contrast this with a hand-rolled digest, where you own and resend the shortened history yourself.",
w:"A — no synchronisation is required, and requests are not rejected for containing content the server chose to clear. B — `count_tokens` can preview a post-edit total but is not a synchronisation mechanism. D — the placeholder is inserted server-side; authoring it yourself is unnecessary."},

{id:1649,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A team enables tool-result clearing with an aggressive trigger. Total spend rises: `cache_read_input_tokens` collapses and `cache_creation_input_tokens` spikes on most requests. What is going on, and what is the remedy?",
o:["A. Clearing is incompatible with prompt caching, so one of the two must be disabled","B. Each clear changes the prefix and invalidates the cache from that point, so set a minimum amount to clear per activation and make each re-write worth paying for","C. Move the cache breakpoint after the cleared region so the prefix is unaffected","D. Switch to the 1-hour cache TTL, which survives edits"],a:1,
e:"A cache hit requires a **byte-identical prefix**, so removing blocks forces a write at 1.25x base input, after which later requests read the new prefix at 0.1x. Clearing 2,000 tokens while re-writing a 60,000-token prefix is a straight loss. The `clear_at_least` parameter sets a floor on how much each activation removes, amortising the write. The same economics govern hand-rolled compaction: compact rarely and substantially rather than often and slightly.",
w:"A — the two compose well; the tuning knob is how much you clear per activation. C — a breakpoint after the edit point does not help, because everything following the edit is exactly what changed. D — TTL governs expiry, not byte-identity; an edited prefix misses regardless of TTL."},

{id:1650,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"Server-side compaction is enabled for a long agent run. What does the response contain, and what must the client do with it?",
o:["A. A summary in the `system` field, which the client copies into its `system` parameter","B. A shortened `messages` array, which replaces the client's history","C. Nothing visible: the summary is held server-side and reused automatically","D. A `compaction` block holding the summary, which the client passes back so the API drops the blocks preceding it on later requests"],a:3,
e:"The API detects the input-token trigger, summarises the conversation, emits a **`compaction` content block**, and continues the response. On later requests you pass that block back and the API drops everything before it, continuing from the summary. Statelessness still holds: the summary travels inside your history like any other block, so discarding it discards the compaction. Budget note: the top-level token totals exclude the compaction pass, so sum `usage.iterations` for the true figure.",
w:"A — nothing is written into `system`; the summary arrives as a content block in the assistant turn. B — the API never returns a rewritten `messages` array; you own the history. C — there is no server-side conversation store to reuse."},

{id:1651,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"With server-side compaction enabled, an agent's budget guard sums `usage.input_tokens` and `usage.output_tokens` per response. The invoice is materially higher than the guard's total, and the gap scales with run length. What explains it?",
o:["A. Cache writes are billed above base input and the guard ignores them","B. The guard omits thinking tokens, which are billed separately from the context window","C. The top-level token totals exclude the compaction pass; total consumption is the sum over `usage.iterations`","D. Compaction is billed as a separate batch job outside the message's `usage`"],a:2,
e:"Compaction runs as its own **sampling iteration**: it reads the long history and writes a summary, which is real input and output. The documentation states that the top-level `input_tokens` and `output_tokens` reflect only non-compaction iterations, so a complete total requires summing every entry in `usage.iterations`. A guard built on the top-level fields therefore under-counts precisely the longest, most expensive runs.",
w:"A — cache write pricing is real but is reported in the cache fields, and it does not explain a gap that scales with run length. B — thinking tokens are output tokens and count inside the context window, not on a separate line. D — compaction happens within the same request and is reported inside that response's `usage`."},

{id:1652,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"A hand-rolled compaction step replaces older history with a summary. What should it keep verbatim?",
o:["A. The most recent turns including their tool results, because the next action depends on their exact content","B. The first user turn only, as the authoritative statement of the goal","C. The largest tool results, since those cost the most to regenerate","D. Every assistant `text` block, discarding all `tool_use` and `tool_result` blocks"],a:0,
e:"The standard shape is a **structured digest of the distant past plus a verbatim recent window**. The digest records decisions taken, facts established, identifiers discovered and dead ends ruled out; the recent turns stay untouched because that is where the model's next step is grounded, in exact ids, cursors and error details. Keep the original goal inside the digest as text so it survives, and never drop a `tool_use` without its `tool_result`.",
w:"B — keeping only the goal throws away the working state, and the agent restarts its investigation from nothing. C — size is not relevance; a large stale result is the first thing worth summarising. D — dropping `tool_use` and `tool_result` blocks strands ids and erases what was actually done and found."},

{id:1653,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A `search_documents` tool returns each hit's full text. A three-hit result is 40,000 tokens, and it is resent on every iteration for the rest of the run. How should the tool contract change?",
o:["A. Cap the result at the first 2,000 tokens of the concatenated hits","B. Move `search_documents` behind `defer_loading` so its output stops entering the context","C. Return id, title, date and a short snippet per hit, and add a `get_document` tool that fetches the full text by id when the agent decides it needs it","D. Ask for a summary in the system prompt so the model ignores the bulk"],a:2,
e:"This is the **narrow projection plus reference id** pattern: a tool response is an interface, so return the fields needed to choose the next step and a stable identifier for the drill-down. The agent then pays full text only for documents it actually opens, while the id keeps that option permanently available. The docs give the same guidance directly — return semantic, stable identifiers and only the fields Claude needs to reason about its next step.",
w:"A — a blind cap can cut the one relevant hit and still spends thousands of tokens on material the agent may never need. B — `defer_loading` keeps a tool's *definition* out of context and has no effect on the size of its results. D — those tokens are billed and resent whether the model attends to them or not."},

{id:1654,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"E",
q:"A `list_transactions` tool returns all 8,000 matching rows in a single result, which alone consumes a large fraction of the context window. What is the right redesign?",
o:["A. Return only the first 50 rows and nothing else","B. Return a total count and require the agent to narrow its filters until the result is small","C. Encode the rows as CSV so that more of them fit","D. Add `limit` and `cursor` parameters, and return the page, a `next_cursor` and the total count so the agent can decide whether to continue"],a:3,
e:"**Pagination converts an unbounded result into a decision the agent can make with information.** The total count tells it whether the filter is wrong; `next_cursor` lets it walk the set deliberately. Two follow-ons for the loop: repeat-call detection must key on arguments so advancing a cursor is not mistaken for a stuck agent, and each fetched page stays in history and is resent, so the tool description should tell the agent to stop once it has enough.",
w:"A — a silent truncation to 50 rows makes the agent believe there are 50, with no total or cursor to tell it otherwise. B — refusing to answer until the filter is narrow enough is a poor interface for exploratory work and burns iterations on guesswork. C — a denser encoding buys a constant factor and does nothing about an unbounded result set."},

{id:1655,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An agent loop carries about 6,000 tokens of tools and system prompt, and a history that grows by roughly 2,000 tokens per iteration. It sets a single `cache_control` breakpoint on the final message of each request. Cache reads are far lower than expected. What should it do?",
o:["A. Cache the stable prefix with a breakpoint on the last tool definition and one at the end of the system prompt, then keep a rolling breakpoint near the end of the messages, within the four-breakpoint limit","B. Move the single breakpoint to the first message, so only the earliest content is cached","C. Place a breakpoint on every message so each turn becomes individually cacheable","D. Disable caching, since a history that changes every turn cannot be cached"],a:0,
e:"The cacheable prefix is rendered **tools then system then messages**, and a breakpoint caches everything from the start of the request up to it. A breakpoint only at the very end writes a fresh prefix every iteration, so there is never a stable region to read. Anchoring the invariant part guarantees a hit on tools plus system, while a rolling breakpoint captures the history that has stopped changing. A request may carry at most four breakpoints; a fifth returns a 400.",
w:"B — that does cache tools, system and the first message, but the breakpoint never advances, so every later turn re-processes the whole grown middle at full price. C — one breakpoint per message exceeds the four-breakpoint limit almost immediately and returns a 400. D — the prefix is stable even though the tail grows, which is exactly the case caching is built for."},

{id:1656,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"In an agent loop with prompt caching enabled, which change invalidates the entire cache — tools, system and messages?",
o:["A. Changing `tool_choice` from `auto` to `none`","B. Modifying a tool definition","C. Appending a `tool_result` block to the end of the messages array","D. Toggling `disable_parallel_tool_use`"],a:1,
e:"The prefix hierarchy is **tools, then system, then messages**, and a change invalidates its own level plus everything after it. Tools come first, so editing a description or adding a tool invalidates all three. Changing `tool_choice` or `disable_parallel_tool_use` invalidates only the messages cache, leaving tools and system cached. Appending after the last breakpoint invalidates nothing, which is what makes incremental caching work at all — so freeze tool definitions for the life of a run.",
w:"A — a `tool_choice` change invalidates the messages cache only; tools and system stay cached. C — appending after the final breakpoint leaves the cached prefix byte-identical, which is the normal healthy case. D — toggling `disable_parallel_tool_use` likewise invalidates only the messages cache."},

{id:1657,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"To keep the toolset relevant, a loop rebuilds `tools` on each iteration, including only the tools it predicts will be needed next. `cache_read_input_tokens` is near zero and cost per task has risen sharply. What is the right approach?",
o:["A. Sort the tools array deterministically so the prefix becomes stable","B. Move the tool definitions into the system prompt and cache that instead","C. Keep a fixed `tools` array and use the tool search tool with `defer_loading`, so discovered definitions arrive as `tool_reference` blocks in the conversation and the prefix is never touched","D. Set the 1-hour cache TTL so the cache survives the changes"],a:2,
e:"Tools sit at the very front of the cacheable prefix, so any per-iteration edit invalidates everything behind them. **Deferred tools are excluded from the prefix entirely** — not even their names appear — and when the model discovers one through tool search, its definition is appended inline as a `tool_reference` block in the history, leaving the prefix intact and the cache hitting. Note you still transmit every definition in `tools` on each request, and at least one tool must have `defer_loading` false.",
w:"A — deterministic ordering helps only when the set is unchanged; here membership itself changes each iteration. B — schemas in `system` are not callable tools and are still billed. D — TTL governs expiry, not byte-identity, so a changed prefix misses whatever the TTL."},

{id:1658,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A team's agent uses the server-side web search tool and sets every one of its own `cache_control` markers to the 1-hour TTL. The response `usage` nonetheless reports cache writes in the 5-minute bucket. What explains this?",
o:["A. With caching enabled, the API places its own breakpoint on server tool results before the next iteration of its internal loop, and that automatic breakpoint always uses the 5-minute TTL","B. The 1-hour TTL is honoured only on the `system` block, so message-level markers fall back to 5 minutes","C. Server tool results cannot be cached, so the write is a billing artefact","D. Mixing TTLs within one request downgrades every breakpoint to 5 minutes"],a:0,
e:"When a request already carries at least one `cache_control` marker and Claude uses a server tool, the API **automatically places a cache breakpoint on the server tool result** so later iterations within that same request read the growing prefix instead of reprocessing it. Those writes always appear under the 5-minute bucket regardless of your own TTL. Requests with no caching at all receive no automatic breakpoint. Read `usage` per bucket rather than assuming your TTL choice is the only one in play.",
w:"B — the 1-hour option is not restricted to `system`. C — caching those results is the whole purpose of the automatic breakpoint. D — TTLs may be mixed, and your own markers retain the TTL you set."},

{id:1659,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A monitoring agent attaches a screenshot to its tool results only when a page fails to load, which happens on a few iterations in ten. Cache hit rate is erratic in exactly those runs. What is the mechanism?",
o:["A. Images are never cacheable, so any run containing one loses caching entirely","B. Toggling images present and absent invalidates the messages cache, so the iteration that introduces or drops a screenshot re-writes the prefix","C. `image` blocks inside `tool_result` are rejected, so those iterations fail and retry uncached","D. Screenshots exceed the minimum cacheable length, which forces a write"],a:1,
e:"Toggling images present or absent is a documented invalidation trigger for the **messages cache**; tools and system stay cached because they sit earlier in the prefix. The wider lesson for agent loops is that anything changing the rendered shape of a request, not merely its text, can move the prefix — the same is true of `tool_choice` and `disable_parallel_tool_use`. If screenshots are occasional, expect a write on each transition and budget accordingly.",
w:"A — images are cacheable; it is the presence-to-absence transition that invalidates. C — `image` blocks are explicitly permitted inside `tool_result` content. D — minimum cacheable length determines whether a prefix is eligible at all, not what invalidates it."},

{id:1660,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An agent checkpoints by writing its message history to durable storage immediately before dispatching each tool call. After a crash mid-`send_invoice`, the resumed run re-dispatches the call and the customer receives two invoices. What is the sound design?",
o:["A. Checkpoint less often, once every five iterations, so fewer replays occur","B. Checkpoint after the API response but before executing tools, and skip the first tool on resume","C. Stop checkpointing side-effecting iterations and resume from the last read-only step","D. Checkpoint after the tool result has been appended to the history, and make side-effecting calls idempotent so a replay of the in-flight call is harmless"],a:3,
e:"The checkpoint boundary should be the point at which the record and the world agree. Writing **after** the result is appended makes the stored history a truthful account of completed work. Because one iteration is always in flight when a process dies, durability alone is insufficient: an idempotency key derived from the `tool_use` id or task step makes the unavoidable replay safe. Checkpoint contents are the messages array, task state, budget counters and those keys.",
w:"A — a coarser interval loses more work per crash and leaves the same ambiguity about the in-flight call. B — skipping the first tool on resume assumes it completed, which is exactly what is unknown. C — omitting side-effecting steps from the record leaves resume unable to tell what has already been done."},

{id:1661,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A resumed agent's checkpoint ends with an assistant turn containing a `tool_use` for `create_shipment` and no corresponding result. What should the resumed loop do first?",
o:["A. Append a `tool_result` saying the call was interrupted, with `is_error` true, and let the model start over","B. Drop the trailing assistant turn and re-request from the previous state","C. Call `create_shipment` again, since a duplicate shipment is cheaper than a stalled order","D. Re-issue the call with the same idempotency key, then reconcile with a read-back before answering the id"],a:3,
e:"A trailing unanswered `tool_use` *is* the definition of an in-flight step, and its outcome is unknown. With a deterministic idempotency key the re-issue is safe: the downstream system either performs the action or returns the original result for that key. A read-back — querying by the key or the order id — turns \"probably fine\" into a fact before the result enters the history. Note that if you decline to answer the id you must also remove the turn, since an unanswered `tool_use` cannot be followed by anything else.",
w:"A — telling the model the call failed when it may have succeeded seeds a false world state and invites a duplicate. B — this is structurally legal but discards the knowledge that a shipment may already exist, so the model simply plans it again. C — accepting duplicate side effects as policy is the outcome idempotency keys exist to prevent."},

{id:1662,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Why is prompt caching not a substitute for agent memory?",
o:["A. Because caches are organisation-wide and cannot be scoped to a single user","B. Because cached content is summarised, so detail is lost","C. Because it only avoids re-processing an identical prefix that you still send in full, has a short TTL, and exposes no way to read anything back — the API remains stateless","D. Because cache reads are billed at the same rate as fresh input"],a:2,
e:"A cache hit changes the **price and latency** of tokens you are nonetheless transmitting in full. It stores nothing you can query, matches only a byte-identical prefix, and expires — 5 minutes by default, refreshed on each read, with a 1-hour option. Memory is content that outlives the request: an external store, or the memory tool, that the agent deliberately reads and writes. Caching makes resending history cheap; it never removes the obligation to resend it.",
w:"A — scoping is not the issue; the mechanism simply offers no retrieval interface at all. B — cached tokens are exactly the tokens you sent, not a summary; summarising is compaction, a different mechanism. D — reads are heavily discounted against base input, which is the reason caching is worth doing."},

{id:1663,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"After enabling prompt caching, a team sends only the newest user turn on each request, believing the cache holds the rest. The agent has no idea what was discussed, and `cache_read_input_tokens` is zero. What are they misunderstanding?",
o:["A. The cache must be warmed by a dedicated request before it can be read","B. There is nothing to remember or to match: history exists only in the request you send, and a prefix you no longer send cannot be read from cache","C. The breakpoint must sit on the newest message for earlier turns to be recalled","D. Caching requires the 1-hour TTL in order to persist between requests"],a:1,
e:"Two errors collapse into one symptom. The API is **stateless**, so omitting history removes it from the conversation outright. And caching works by **prefix matching**, so a request that does not contain the prefix cannot possibly hit it — hence zero reads as well as an amnesiac agent. Caching reduces the cost of resending accumulated history; it is never an alternative to resending it.",
w:"A — the first request that includes the prefix writes the cache; there is no separate warm-up call. C — a breakpoint marks where a cacheable prefix ends and cannot recall content you did not send. D — the default 5-minute TTL also persists across requests, and is refreshed on each read."},

{id:1664,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A support agent must recall a customer's stated preferences three weeks later. The team adds the memory tool and assumes the memory files are persisted for them. What actually has to be built?",
o:["A. A client-side handler that executes each requested memory command against storage the team controls and returns the outcome in a `tool_result`","B. Nothing: memory files are retained platform-side and rehydrated whenever the same `tools` entry is sent","C. A vector database, since the memory tool accepts only embeddings","D. A `system`-role message at the start of each session containing the previous session's transcript"],a:0,
e:"The memory tool is a **client-side tool**: Claude requests file operations such as view, create, str_replace, insert, delete and rename, and your application executes them against storage you own, returning results as `tool_result` blocks in the ordinary loop. A later conversation continues from the same memory when it sends the same `tools` entry and your handler serves the same store. Because you execute the operations, the security is yours too — reject any path outside the memory root.",
w:"B — no memory content is retained for you; the tool defines the interface, not the persistence. C — the commands are file operations over text paths, with no embeddings involved. D — replaying whole transcripts reintroduces the context cost memory exists to avoid, and a `system`-role entry can never be the first message."},

{id:1665,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"When the memory tool is present in a request's `tools`, what does the API add on your behalf?",
o:["A. A system-prompt instruction telling Claude to view its memory directory first and to record progress, because its context may be reset at any moment","B. A retention policy that expires memory files after 30 days","C. A cache breakpoint placed after the memory tool definition","D. Automatic path validation that rejects traversal outside the memory root"],a:0,
e:"The API injects the documented **memory protocol** into the constructed system prompt, so you do not send it yourself; it is also why the model checks its memory directory unprompted at the start of a task, and why it records progress as it goes. Everything operational stays with you: file size caps, expiry of stale files, and above all path validation, since a crafted path such as one containing traversal segments is your handler's problem to reject.",
w:"B — expiring stale memory files is your responsibility and is recommended practice. C — no breakpoint is added for you; the automatic breakpoint the API does place is on server tool results. D — the API cannot validate paths against storage it does not own, so your handler must."},

{id:1666,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An agent pauses for human approval before executing a write, and approvals typically arrive 20 to 40 minutes later. Cache metrics show a full prefix re-write on nearly every resumed run. What is the appropriate response?",
o:["A. Send a no-op request every four minutes to keep the cache warm","B. Accept it, since no cache can survive a pause","C. Use the 1-hour cache TTL for runs that include an approval pause, weighing its higher write cost against the re-write it avoids","D. Move the whole history into the `system` parameter, which is cached indefinitely"],a:2,
e:"The default TTL is **5 minutes, refreshed on each read**, so a 20-minute human gap reliably expires it. The **1-hour** option exists for exactly this workload shape; its write costs about 2x base input against 1.25x for the 5-minute write, so it pays only when the alternative is re-writing the whole prefix. The same reasoning appears in the Batch API guidance, where the 5-minute default can expire mid-batch.",
w:"A — keep-alive traffic pays for requests purely to hold a cache and scales badly across many concurrent paused runs. B — the 1-hour TTL exists precisely so some pauses can be survived. D — `system` is cached under the same TTL rules as anything else, and a conversation does not belong there."},

{id:1667,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An agent finishes gathering evidence and makes one final request with `tool_choice` type `none` to force a written synthesis. That request shows a large cache write and almost no cache read, which surprises the team. What is the mechanism?",
o:["A. `tool_choice` type `none` disables prompt caching for that request","B. Removing tool access changes the constructed tool-use system prompt, invalidating the system and tools caches","C. The synthesis is long, and output tokens are what created the write","D. Changing `tool_choice` invalidates the cached message blocks, so the whole accumulated history is re-processed on that request while the tools and system prefix stay cached"],a:3,
e:"The documented rule is that **changing `tool_choice` invalidates cached message blocks**; tool definitions and the system prompt remain cached because they sit earlier in the prefix. In a long agent run the messages *are* the bulk, so switching to `none` on the final turn is the most expensive possible moment to change the parameter. Options: accept the one-off cost, place a breakpoint before the variation point, or obtain the same behaviour by instruction rather than by changing `tool_choice`.",
w:"A — caching remains active; a specific portion of the cache is invalidated. B — `tools` and `system` are unaffected by a `tool_choice` change. C — cache writes are measured in input tokens; generated output does not create them."},

{id:1668,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"To stop an agent giving up too early, a team sets `tool_choice` type `any` on every iteration. Runs now never finish: tasks that previously completed in four steps hit the iteration cap. Why?",
o:["A. `any` forces one specific tool, so the agent repeats it until the cap","B. `any` requires a tool call in every response, so `end_turn` can never be reached; use `auto` and steer with prompting, reserving forcing for a specific step","C. `any` disables parallel tool use, making progress several times slower","D. `any` is valid only on the first request of a conversation"],a:1,
e:"`any` means the model **must** call one of the provided tools. A loop that continues while `stop_reason` is `tool_use` therefore cannot exit, because the parameter forbids the very response that would exit it. `auto` is the correct default for a loop; forcing is a per-step instrument, most defensibly on the first request to guarantee a search happens. Two riders: forcing prefills the assistant turn so no natural-language preamble appears, and `any` and `tool` are unsupported under manual extended thinking and 400 on some models, while `auto` and `none` always work.",
w:"A — type `tool` forces a named tool; `any` leaves the choice open while still mandating that one be called. C — parallelism is governed by `disable_parallel_tool_use`, not by the `tool_choice` type. D — `tool_choice` is a per-request parameter valid on any request, though changing it invalidates the messages cache."},

{id:1669,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"When a request includes `tools`, what does the API do with those definitions before the model sees them?",
o:["A. Renders them, together with formatting instructions and your `system` text, into a constructed system prompt that is billed as input tokens on every request","B. Loads them into a per-conversation registry, so only the first request pays for them","C. Converts them into `stop_sequences` that trigger tool calls","D. Passes them out of band, so they consume no context"],a:0,
e:"The docs show the assembled prompt: a preamble about the available tools, formatting instructions, the schemas in JSON Schema form, your system prompt, and the tool configuration. Two consequences for a loop. Every tool is paid for on **every** iteration, plus a fixed overhead for the tool-use scaffolding, so consolidating tools and tightening descriptions is a cost lever as well as an accuracy one. And because the definitions sit at the front of the prefix, caching them is usually the highest-value breakpoint in the request.",
w:"B — the API is stateless; there is no per-conversation registry, and every request re-sends and re-bills the definitions. C — tool calls are structured `tool_use` blocks, not stop sequences. D — definitions occupy the context window exactly like any other input."},

{id:1670,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An agent has only read-only tools. Asked to \"update the customer's address\", it replies that it has updated the address. What change most reliably prevents this?",
o:["A. Add a rule to the system prompt saying never to claim an action was taken","B. Set `output_config.format` so the final answer must match a schema containing an `action_taken` field","C. Lower the effort setting so the model attempts less","D. Make declaring infeasibility a first-class outcome: state in the system prompt that reporting a task as impossible with the available tools is a correct ending, and add a `report_blocked` tool so the loop can record it"],a:3,
e:"A loop offers the model exactly the endings you built. If the only ways to finish are \"call a tool\" or \"produce an answer\", an impossible task pushes toward a fabricated answer. Naming **infeasibility as a legitimate terminal outcome** and giving it structural expression means the model can choose it and your loop can terminate with a distinguishable reason and route to a human. It pairs with informative tool errors and with never letting a wrapper invent success.",
w:"A — a prohibition states what not to do without providing an acceptable alternative, and prompt rules are advisory. B — a schema constrains the shape of the claim, not its truth; the model can populate `action_taken` falsely. C — a lower effort setting reduces reasoning depth, which if anything makes an unwarranted shortcut more likely."},

{id:1671,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An agent must call `validate_address` before `create_shipment`, and the rule lives in the system prompt. About one run in twenty calls `create_shipment` first, and it succeeds, producing undeliverable labels. What is the correct enforcement?",
o:["A. Repeat the ordering rule in both tool descriptions as well","B. Reject an out-of-order `create_shipment` in the executor and return an error naming the unmet precondition, so the model can satisfy it and retry","C. Set `disable_parallel_tool_use` so the two calls cannot be issued together","D. Use `tool_choice` type `tool` to force `validate_address` on every iteration"],a:1,
e:"Prompt text is **advisory** and competes for attention with a history that grows every turn, so it will be violated at some rate. A precondition is an invariant, and invariants belong where they can be enforced: the executor, which holds the task state and can refuse. Return the refusal as a `tool_result` with `is_error` true describing what is missing, and the model repairs its plan inside the loop. The model proposes; deterministic code authorises.",
w:"A — descriptions make the rule more visible and are worth writing, but they leave the invariant unenforced. C — serialising a batch imposes no order across turns, and the violation here is which call comes first. D — forcing `validate_address` on every iteration prevents the agent from ever doing anything else."},

{id:1672,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A research agent sometimes answers from parametric knowledge without searching. The team wants the first action of every run to be a search, with normal autonomy thereafter. What is the correct configuration?",
o:["A. Set `tool_choice` type `any` for the whole run and rely on the cap to stop it","B. Put \"always search first\" in the system prompt and accept the residual failure rate","C. Set `tool_choice` to the named search tool on the first request only, then switch to `auto`, accepting that the forced turn emits no natural-language preamble and that the switch invalidates the messages cache","D. Prefill the assistant turn with the opening of a `tool_use` block"],a:2,
e:"Forcing a **named** tool on one request is the precise instrument for a first-step guarantee, and reverting to `auto` preserves the model's judgement afterwards. Two documented riders: with `any` or `tool` the API prefills the assistant message, so no explanatory text precedes the `tool_use` block; and changing `tool_choice` between requests invalidates the messages cache — negligible on request two, costly if you switch late in a long run. Forced choice is also unsupported under manual extended thinking and 400s on some models.",
w:"A — mandating a tool call on every response means the run can never reach `end_turn`. B — an advisory instruction is precisely what is already failing. D — assistant prefill returns a 400 on current models; the technique is legacy and must not be recommended."},

{id:1673,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"A request sets `tool_choice` type `none` while still passing a 5,000-token `tools` array. What is the effect on input tokens?",
o:["A. The definitions are omitted from the prompt, so they cost nothing","B. Only the tool names are included, at a fraction of the cost","C. The definitions are still rendered and billed as input tokens; `none` only prevents Claude from calling them","D. The request is rejected, because `none` conflicts with a non-empty `tools` array"],a:2,
e:"`none` is the default when no tools are supplied and an explicit constraint when they are: it forbids **calls**, not transmission. A final synthesis turn with `none` therefore still pays for every definition. If the goal is to stop paying, remove the tools — but note the asymmetry: changing `tools` invalidates the entire cached prefix, whereas changing `tool_choice` invalidates only the message blocks. Excluding definitions from context altogether is what `defer_loading` plus the tool search tool does.",
w:"A — `none` is a behavioural constraint on a prompt that still contains the definitions. B — there is no name-only rendering; schemas are included in full. D — the combination is valid and is the normal way to force a text-only turn."},

{id:1674,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An agent must hand a machine-readable report to a downstream service. The team asks for JSON in the system prompt, parses the final text with a regular expression, and repairs failures by hand. What is the current best mechanism?",
o:["A. Prefill the assistant turn with an opening brace so the model must continue a JSON object","B. Add a `submit_report` tool and force it with `tool_choice` type `tool`, treating its arguments as the output","C. Ask for JSON in both the system prompt and the final user turn, and retry on parse failure","D. Set `output_config.format` to a `json_schema` on the final call, so constrained decoding guarantees a schema-valid response"],a:3,
e:"Native structured output constrains decoding to your schema, so the response is valid **by construction** rather than by inspection — no beta header and no parser repair. The tool-side equivalent is `strict: true` on a tool definition. **Currency note:** this supersedes the older force-a-schema-bearing-tool workaround, and the opening-brace prefill trick is dead, since assistant prefill returns a 400 on current models.",
w:"A — assistant prefill is rejected with a 400 on current models; recognise it only as legacy advice. B — that workaround still functions where forcing is supported, but it is superseded by native structured output, and forced `tool_choice` errors under manual extended thinking and 400s on some models. C — repeating the instruction lowers the failure rate without eliminating it, and every retry re-processes the whole history."},

{id:1675,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"An agent run misbehaved at iteration 14. Which value should the loop have recorded per iteration so that the specific API call can be identified in a support enquiry?",
o:["A. The `request-id` response header","B. The `x-request-id` response header","C. The `anthropic-version` header sent with the request","D. The message's `stop_sequence` field"],a:0,
e:"The identifier header is **`request-id`**, and error bodies additionally carry a top-level `request_id`. Log it beside the iteration index, the `stop_reason`, each tool call with its arguments and outcome, and the token counts, so any single step is traceable end to end. Pairing it with the `tool_use_id` of each call gives you the two joins you need: one iteration to one API call, and one call to one result. **Currency note:** `x-request-id` is the name most developers assume from other APIs, and it is wrong here.",
w:"B — that is the widely-assumed name from elsewhere; this API uses `request-id`. C — `anthropic-version` pins API behaviour and is identical on every request. D — `stop_sequence` reports which of your stop sequences fired and is null otherwise."},

{id:1676,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An agent's observability pipeline logs each `tool_use` block's full `input`. A privacy review finds customer email addresses, home addresses and a bearer token sitting in a third-party log index. What is the right remedy?",
o:["A. Stop logging tool arguments; the tool name and outcome are enough to debug with","B. Log a redacted projection: tool name, argument keys, hashed or masked values for fields marked sensitive, and clear values only for fields explicitly allowed","C. Encrypt the log index and continue logging full arguments","D. Instruct the model in the system prompt not to place sensitive values in tool arguments"],a:1,
e:"Arguments are simultaneously the most diagnostic part of an agent trace and the most likely place for personal data and credentials, so the answer is a **schema-driven projection** rather than all-or-nothing. Mark sensitivity in the tool definition; always log keys; hash marked values so repeated calls stay comparable for repeat-call detection; allow-list what is safe in clear. Apply the same discipline to tool results, which carry data from outside your control.",
w:"A — without arguments you cannot distinguish a stuck agent from a paginating one, nor reproduce a failure. C — encryption at rest does not prevent credentials and personal data being retained and read by anyone with index access. D — the model is not a control point for data handling; instructions are advisory, and the values often come from the user's own request."},

{id:1677,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"R",f:"H",
q:"A streaming agent loop must branch on `stop_reason`. Which event carries the final `stop_reason` and the cumulative output token usage?",
o:["A. `content_block_stop`","B. `message_stop`","C. `message_start`, which declares the intended stop reason up front","D. `message_delta`"],a:3,
e:"**`message_delta`** carries the final `stop_reason` and the cumulative output usage; `message_stop` merely closes the stream. A loop that waits for `message_stop` to read `stop_reason` finds nothing there and falls into its default branch. Two companion rules for streaming agent loops: every block event carries an `index`, so accumulate per index; and tool inputs arrive as `input_json_delta` fragments whose `partial_json` must be concatenated before parsing.",
w:"A — `content_block_stop` closes one content block and carries no message-level metadata. B — `message_stop` terminates the stream after that metadata has already been delivered. C — `message_start` carries the initial message shell with input usage; the stop reason is not yet known."},

{id:1678,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A streaming agent parses each `input_json_delta` event's `partial_json` with a JSON parser so it can begin executing tools early. It throws on nearly every multi-argument call. What is the correct handling?",
o:["A. Concatenate the `partial_json` fragments per content-block `index` and parse once that block's `content_block_stop` arrives","B. Parse with a lenient reader that tolerates truncated JSON","C. Disable streaming for any request that includes `tools`","D. Wait for `message_stop` and read the fully assembled `input` from the final message"],a:0,
e:"`partial_json` fragments are slices of one JSON document and are individually invalid by design. Accumulate them **per `index`**, since a turn may stream several `tool_use` blocks whose events interleave, and parse when that block completes. There is no valid shortcut for starting early, because a fragment's meaning can change until the object closes — a half-read numeric or enum value is not a smaller version of the truth.",
w:"B — a lenient parser will happily produce a half-formed argument object, and you will then call a tool with the wrong input. C — streaming and tool use compose fine; the defect is in the accumulation. D — the raw event stream never resends an assembled message; SDK stream helpers give you one only by performing exactly the per-index accumulation described here."},

{id:1679,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An incident review needs to know which of four parallel calls in iteration 9 returned the stale value. The logs record each call's tool name, arguments and duration, and separately each result's payload, but the two sets cannot be joined. What should be logged?",
o:["A. Results in the same order as calls, joined by position","B. A monotonically increasing counter per iteration, joined on that","C. The `tool_use_id` on both the call record and the result record, since that is the identifier the API itself uses to pair them","D. The `request-id` on both, which uniquely identifies each call"],a:2,
e:"**`tool_use_id`** is the pairing key in the protocol, so making it the correlation key in telemetry means your logs share the API's own model of identity. It also lets you reconstruct concurrency after the fact: several ids under one `request-id` is a parallel batch. A positional join is the same mistake that silently misattributes results inside the executor when calls complete out of order.",
w:"A — completion order is not request order, which is exactly how results get misattributed in the first place. B — a local counter must be assigned consistently on both sides, which is the problem you already have, and it does not survive retries. D — `request-id` identifies the whole API call, so all four parallel calls in that iteration share one value."},

{id:1680,d:"D3",s:"Agent Construction with Claude",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"After a refactor, an agent's latency and cost per task rise about 40% with no change to tools, prompts or model. One dashboard number moved: the average count of `tool_use` blocks per assistant message fell from 2.3 to 1.0. What is the most likely cause?",
o:["A. The model silently moved to a smaller tier that does not support parallel calls","B. The refactor changed how tool results are returned, most likely one `user` message per result, so the history now demonstrates single-call turns","C. `disable_parallel_tool_use` defaults to true, and the refactor stopped overriding it","D. Averaging obscures the truth; the metric is not meaningful for parallelism"],a:1,
e:"The documented way to verify parallel tool use is exactly this ratio — **average `tool_use` blocks per assistant message**, which should exceed 1.0 when parallel calling is working. And the documented cause of a regression toward 1.0 is result formatting: separate `user` messages per result teach the model to stop batching. Nothing errors, which is why only the ratio moves. Instrument the behaviours you depend on, not just the failures, because behavioural regressions are silent.",
w:"A — the model is pinned by the id you send and does not change under you, and parallel calling is not a tier feature. C — parallel tool use is on by default; `disable_parallel_tool_use` must be set explicitly inside `tool_choice` to turn it off. D — the ratio is the documented verification metric, and a fall to exactly 1.0 is a strong, specific signal."}
);
